// Generated by packages/academy/scripts/build-content.ts. Don't edit by hand.
export default JSON.parse("{\"3.8.1\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.1\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABA reports record quarterly profit: net income of 60m vUSD. Yet its cash fell by 45m. What is the most likely reason?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"error\",\"label\":\"The report contains an error\",\"feedback\":\"Profit and cash often differ in a correct report. The cash-flow statement shows exactly where they parted.\"},{\"id\":\"credit\",\"label\":\"Customers haven't paid yet, and it bought equipment\"},{\"id\":\"price\",\"label\":\"The share price fell\",\"feedback\":\"The share price is set by the market. It doesn't touch the company's own cash.\"},{\"id\":\"tax\",\"label\":\"It paid its taxes twice\",\"feedback\":\"Rare, and it would show up as a cost. Check the ordinary reasons first: unpaid sales, stock and equipment.\"}],\"answer\":\"credit\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Net income 60 + depreciation 20 − receivables up 80 − inventory up 30 = operating cash flow −30m. Minus capex 15m: free cash flow −45m. No new debt or shares, so cash fell 45m.\",\"concept\":\"financial-statements\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"One number, three statements\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"flow\",\"start\":\"is\",\"nodes\":[{\"id\":\"is\",\"text\":\"Income statement: revenue − costs = net income, 60m\",\"kind\":\"pause\",\"next\":\"bs\"},{\"id\":\"bs\",\"text\":\"Balance sheet: retained earnings, part of equity, rise by 60m\",\"kind\":\"pause\",\"next\":\"cf\"},{\"id\":\"cf\",\"text\":\"Cash-flow statement: 60m + depreciation − rise in receivables and inventory\",\"kind\":\"pause\",\"next\":\"capex\"},{\"id\":\"capex\",\"text\":\"Capex: PP&E rises on the balance sheet; cash leaves as investing cash flow\",\"kind\":\"end\"}],\"steps\":[\"The income statement turns a quarter's revenue into net income: 60m.\",\"Net income not paid out lands in retained earnings, part of equity on the balance sheet.\",\"The cash-flow statement starts from 60m, adds back depreciation and subtracts the rise in receivables and inventory.\",\"Capex raises PP&E on the balance sheet and leaves as investing cash flow.\"]},\"text\":\"The [[income-statement]] covers a period, the [[balance-sheet]] is a snapshot on one date, and the [[cash-flow-statement]] shows the cash that actually moved. Net income is the thread that ties all three.\",\"concept\":\"financial-statements\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"From profit to cash\",\"lines\":[\"Net income: +60m.\",\"Depreciation is a cost that spent no cash this quarter: add back +20m.\",\"Receivables rose 80m: sales booked, cash not yet collected: −80m.\",\"Inventory rose 30m: cash spent on goods still unsold: −30m.\",\"[[operating-cash-flow|Operating cash flow]] = 60 + 20 − 80 − 30 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-30,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"m\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":30,\"text\":\"Check the sign. Receivables and inventory both rose, so they took cash away.\"},{\"value\":190,\"text\":\"Rising receivables and inventory are cash going out. Subtract them.\"},{\"value\":-70,\"text\":\"Depreciation is added back: it lowered profit without spending cash.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: −30m. The business made a profit and still burned cash.\",\"incorrect\":\"Start at 60, add 20, then subtract 80 and 30.\"},\"after\":\"Then subtract the 15m of [[capex]]: [[free-cash-flow]] = −30 − 15 = −45m. With no new debt or shares, that's exactly the fall in cash.\",\"concept\":\"free-cash-flow\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Where it shows up first\",\"prompt\":\"Match each transaction with the first line it changes.\",\"columns\":[\"Transaction\",\"First line changed\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Sell 10m of goods on credit\",\"right\":\"Receivables\",\"wrong\":\"A credit sale is revenue now and cash later. Until the customer pays, it sits in receivables.\"},{\"left\":\"Buy a 15m machine\",\"right\":\"Capex and PP&E\",\"wrong\":\"A machine lasts years. Cash leaves now as capex; profit feels it slowly, through depreciation.\"},{\"left\":\"Pay a 5m dividend\",\"right\":\"Financing cash flow and retained earnings\",\"wrong\":\"A dividend isn't a cost. It's a payout to owners: it cuts retained earnings and leaves as financing cash flow.\"},{\"left\":\"Issue 20m of new shares\",\"right\":\"Financing cash flow and equity\",\"wrong\":\"New shares bring cash in from investors. That's financing, not operations, and it raises equity.\"}],\"concept\":\"financial-statements\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Why profit and cash part ways\",\"text\":\"Profit counts a sale when it's made; cash counts it when it's paid. Growing receivables and inventory, and heavy [[capex]], push cash below profit. Collecting old receivables or cutting inventory does the reverse. Net income divided by the share count is [[eps]], the headline number, and it inherits all of these gaps.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Run LABA for a quarter\",\"prompt\":\"Quarter 1: LABA sells 400m, all for cash, and earns 48m (12%); free cash flow is 48m. Which quarter-2 plan raises net income but turns free cash flow negative?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"capex\",\"label\":\"All cash sales; buy a 40m machine\",\"feedback\":\"Profit stays at 48m: the machine reaches it slowly, through depreciation. Free cash flow falls to 8m, still positive.\"},{\"id\":\"credit\",\"label\":\"Sell 80% on credit; buy a 40m machine\"},{\"id\":\"shares\",\"label\":\"All cash sales; issue 50m of new shares\",\"feedback\":\"Cash rises 98m, but 50m of it is financing. Net income and free cash flow both stay at 48m.\"},{\"id\":\"both\",\"label\":\"Issue 50m of shares; buy a 40m machine\",\"feedback\":\"The shares pay for the machine. Net income stays 48m, free cash flow is 8m, and cash still rises.\"}],\"answer\":\"credit\"},\"reveal\":\"Credit wins buyers: revenue rises to 450m and net income to 54m. But a third of the credit sales are still unpaid at the quarter's end, so receivables rise 120m. Add the 40m machine and free cash flow turns deeply negative. Next, the sum.\",\"concept\":\"free-cash-flow\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The plan, line by line\",\"lines\":[\"Revenue 450m × 12% margin = net income 54m, up 6m.\",\"Credit sales: 80% × 450 = 360m. A third is unpaid at quarter-end: receivables +120m.\",\"Operating cash flow = 54 − 120 = −66m.\",\"Capex 40m. Free cash flow = −66 − 40 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-106,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"m\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":-66,\"text\":\"That's operating cash flow. Subtract the 40m of capex.\"},{\"value\":14,\"text\":\"54 − 40 skips the receivables. Unpaid sales are profit without cash.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: −106m. Profit up 6m; free cash flow down from +48m to −106m.\",\"incorrect\":\"Operating cash flow −66m, minus capex 40m.\"},\"after\":\"Profit up, cash down, from ordinary decisions. Nothing in the report is wrong; the cash-flow statement shows what the profit line hides. If customers pay next quarter, cash catches up.\",\"concept\":\"free-cash-flow\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Read the cash-flow statement next to the profit line. Net income rising while operating cash flow falls is a question to answer: how much cash is tied up in receivables and inventory, and will it come back?\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.1-1\",\"q-3.8.1-2\",\"q-3.8.1-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Net income links the statements: it feeds retained earnings and starts the cash-flow statement.\",\"Operating cash flow adds back depreciation and subtracts rising receivables and inventory; FCF subtracts capex.\",\"Credit sales and capex can lift profit while free cash flow turns negative.\"]}},\"3.8.2\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.2\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABA and LABB both report adjusted EPS of 2.00. LABB pays staff mostly in new shares, so its share count grows 6% a year, and it spends heavily on equipment. Which earns more cash per share?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"laba\",\"label\":\"LABA\"},{\"id\":\"labb\",\"label\":\"LABB\",\"feedback\":\"LABB's 2.00 leaves out its share-based pay, and equipment spending eats its cash. Per share, it keeps far less.\"},{\"id\":\"same\",\"label\":\"The same\",\"feedback\":\"Same adjusted EPS, very different cash. Adjusted EPS leaves out whatever each company chose to leave out.\"}],\"answer\":\"laba\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. LABA: free cash flow 210m ÷ 100m shares = 2.10 a share. LABB: operating cash flow 240m − capex 160m = 80m, or 0.80 a share, and next year 6% more shares split it.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-quality\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Two EPS numbers\",\"lines\":[\"LABB's adjusted EPS: 2.00. Its GAAP EPS, under the accounting rules: 1.10.\",\"The 0.90 gap: share-based pay 0.70, plus 'one-off' restructuring costs 0.20.\",\"Share-based pay is a real cost, paid in new shares instead of cash: it shows up as [[dilution]].\",\"The 'one-off' costs have appeared in each of the last four years.\"],\"after\":\"[[adjusted-eps|Adjusted EPS]] is the company's own definition. Read what it leaves out: a cost that comes back every year isn't one-off, and paying in shares is still paying.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-quality\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Growth from a buyback\",\"lines\":[\"LABD's net income: 100m last year and 100m this year.\",\"A [[share-buyback]] cuts its shares from 100m to 95m.\",\"EPS: 100 ÷ 100 = 1.00 last year; 100 ÷ 95 = 1.053 this year.\",\"EPS growth, in % = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":5.3,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":5,\"text\":\"5m is 5% of the old share count, but EPS divides by the new, smaller count: 1.053 ÷ 1.00.\"},{\"value\":0,\"text\":\"Profit growth is 0%. EPS growth isn't: the same profit over fewer shares.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: +5.3% EPS growth. Business growth: 0%.\",\"incorrect\":\"1.053 ÷ 1.00 − 1, in %.\"},\"after\":\"Business growth: 0%. The buyback raised EPS by shrinking the denominator. That isn't bad, but it isn't the business growing, and if borrowed money paid for it, risk went up too.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-quality\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"What each ratio measures\",\"prompt\":\"Match each ratio with what it tells you.\",\"columns\":[\"Ratio\",\"Measures\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"[[roe|ROE]]: net income ÷ equity\",\"right\":\"Profit on each unit of the owners' money\",\"wrong\":\"ROE puts profit over equity, the owners' stake. Debt can inflate it by shrinking that stake.\"},{\"left\":\"[[net-debt|Net debt]] ÷ EBITDA\",\"right\":\"Years of operating earnings needed to repay the debt\",\"wrong\":\"Net debt over EBITDA, operating profit before depreciation, is roughly how many years of earnings the debt equals.\"},{\"left\":\"[[interest-coverage|Interest coverage]]: EBIT ÷ interest\",\"right\":\"How many times operating profit pays the interest bill\",\"wrong\":\"Coverage divides operating profit by interest. Near 1, any dip in profit leaves the interest unpaid.\"},{\"left\":\"Inventory days\",\"right\":\"How long goods sit before they're sold\",\"wrong\":\"Inventory days measure how long stock waits on the shelf. Rising faster than sales means goods are piling up.\"}],\"concept\":\"earnings-quality\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"LABD's ROE is 20%: net income 100m ÷ equity 500m. But debt funds 2,000m of its 2,500m of assets, a debt-to-equity ratio of 4. On its assets it earns 4%. That 20% is borrowed: a rate rise or a weak year hits it first.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Three companies, eight ratios\",\"lines\":[\"LABA: ROE 18%, ROIC 16%, net debt/EBITDA 0.4×, coverage 22×, current ratio 1.8, inventory days 40 → 41, receivable days 35, FCF margin 14%.\",\"LABD: ROE 20%, ROIC 7%, net debt/EBITDA 4.8×, coverage 1.9×, current ratio 0.9, inventory days 37 → 38, receivable days 40, FCF margin 5%.\",\"LABE: ROE 11%, ROIC 10%, net debt/EBITDA 1.2×, coverage 9×, current ratio 2.4, inventory days 60 → 95, receivable days 58, FCF margin −3%. Sales grew 4%.\"],\"after\":\"ROIC is the return on all the capital, debt included. Arrows show last year → this year.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-quality\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Tag the three\",\"prompt\":\"From the table, tag the levered company, the one with an inventory problem and the steady compounder.\",\"columns\":[\"Company\",\"Tag\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"LABA\",\"right\":\"Steady compounder\",\"wrong\":\"LABA: high returns on capital, little debt, flat inventory days and real free cash flow.\"},{\"left\":\"LABD\",\"right\":\"Levered\",\"wrong\":\"LABD's 20% ROE sits on a 7% ROIC, net debt of 4.8× EBITDA and interest covered just 1.9×. The return is borrowed.\"},{\"left\":\"LABE\",\"right\":\"Inventory problem\",\"wrong\":\"LABE's inventory days jumped from 60 to 95 while sales grew 4%: goods are piling up, and free cash flow turned negative.\"}],\"correct\":\"Leverage shows in net debt/EBITDA and coverage, not in ROE. Inventory days rising faster than sales is the warning sign.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-quality\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.2-1\",\"q-3.8.2-2\",\"q-3.8.2-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Rank by free cash flow per share: adjusted EPS hides recurring costs, dilution and capex.\",\"A buyback lifts EPS with the same profit; that growth isn't the business growing.\",\"Leverage shows in net debt/EBITDA and interest coverage, not in a high ROE.\"]}},\"3.8.3\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.3\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Discount rates rise from 8% to 9%. LABB grows fast, LABA slowly; both earn the same cash today. Which stock falls more, and by roughly how much?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"b33\",\"label\":\"LABB, about −33%\"},{\"id\":\"b12\",\"label\":\"LABB, about −12%\",\"feedback\":\"LABB does fall more, by far more than 12%: its price goes from 50 times its cash to 33 times.\"},{\"id\":\"a33\",\"label\":\"LABA, about −33%\",\"feedback\":\"The slow grower's cash arrives sooner, so a higher rate hurts it less: −12.5%.\"},{\"id\":\"same\",\"label\":\"Both about −11%\",\"feedback\":\"One point of rate isn't one fixed percentage. The closer growth is to the rate, the bigger the hit.\"}],\"answer\":\"b33\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. With price = cash ÷ (r − g): LABB, g = 6%, goes from 1 ÷ 0.02 = 50 to 1 ÷ 0.03 = 33.33, −33%. LABA, g = 1%, goes from 14.29 to 12.50, −12.5%.\",\"concept\":\"equity-duration\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Price multiples and EV\",\"lines\":[\"LABD: price 40.00, EPS 2.00. [[pe-ratio|P/E]] = 40 ÷ 2 = 20.\",\"100m shares × 40.00 = market cap 4,000m.\",\"Add net debt of 1,000m: [[enterprise-value|enterprise value]] = 5,000m, the price of the whole business.\",\"EBITDA, operating profit before depreciation: 500m. [[ev-ebitda|EV/EBITDA]] = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":10,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"×\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":8,\"text\":\"4,000 ÷ 500 uses the market cap alone. EV adds the 1,000m of net debt.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 10×. A buyer of the whole company pays 5,000m: the shares plus the debt it takes on.\",\"incorrect\":\"EV ÷ EBITDA = 5,000 ÷ 500.\"},\"after\":\"P/E looks only at the shares. EV/EBITDA looks at the whole business, debt included, so it compares companies with different balance sheets.\",\"concept\":\"valuation-multiples\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Cheaper, or financed differently?\",\"prompt\":\"LABA has the same market cap, 4,000m, and the same EBITDA, 500m, but 1,000m of net cash instead of net debt. Which business is cheaper?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"laba\",\"label\":\"LABA\"},{\"id\":\"labd\",\"label\":\"LABD\",\"feedback\":\"LABD's buyer also takes on 1,000m of debt. Its whole business costs 5,000m, not 4,000m.\"},{\"id\":\"same\",\"label\":\"Neither: same market cap, same EBITDA\",\"feedback\":\"Market cap ignores debt and cash. Buy all of LABA's shares and 1,000m of cash comes with them.\"}],\"answer\":\"laba\"},\"reveal\":\"LABA. Buy all its shares for 4,000m and 1,000m of cash comes with them: the business costs 3,000m, 6× EBITDA, against LABD's 10×. Market cap ÷ EBITDA says 8× for both. 'Cheaper' still isn't 'undervalued': find out why before calling it a bargain.\",\"concept\":\"valuation-multiples\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Same rate rise, different falls\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100}],\"hideCandles\":true,\"range\":[30,105],\"describe\":\"Price against the discount rate from 8% to 12%, both set to 100 at 8%. Growth 1%: 100, 93.33, 87.50, 82.35, 77.78, 73.68, 70.00, 66.67, 63.64. Growth 6%: 100, 80.00, 66.67, 57.14, 50.00, 44.44, 40.00, 36.36, 33.33.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Both stocks set to 100 at an 8% rate. Each slot right adds half a point.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"100 at 8%\"}]},{\"text\":\"Slow grower, g = 1%: 87.50 at 9%, 63.64 at 12%.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[100,93.33,87.5,82.35,77.78,73.68,70,66.67,63.64],\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"g = 1%\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":87.5,\"label\":\"9%\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},{\"text\":\"Fast grower, g = 6%: 66.67 at 9%, 33.33 at 12%. A far steeper curve.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[100,80,66.67,57.14,50,44.44,40,36.36,33.33],\"role\":\"accent\",\"label\":\"g = 6%\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":66.67,\"label\":\"9%\",\"role\":\"accent\"}]}]},\"text\":\"Equity duration: the further away a company's cash lies, the more a change in the [[discount-rate]] moves its value. Fast growers' cash lies years ahead, so they behave like long bonds. That's how rate news reaches stock prices: through the multiples.\",\"concept\":\"equity-duration\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Cheap can stay cheap. A low P/E can mean slow growth, high risk or decline: a [[value-trap]]. A cyclical company's P/E looks high at the bottom of its cycle, because profit is depressed. Multiples are poor short-term timing tools (grade D); value as a long-run factor is graded A/B, with long dry spells.\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"Rank the rate shock\",\"prompt\":\"Four stocks earn the same cash today. Expected growth: LABA 1%, LABD 3%, LABE 5%, LABB 6.5%. The discount rate rises from 8% to 9%. Order them from the biggest fall to the smallest.\",\"items\":[\"LABB, growth 6.5%\",\"LABE, growth 5%\",\"LABD, growth 3%\",\"LABA, growth 1%\"],\"explain\":\"By growth, which here means by duration: LABB −40%, LABE −25%, LABD −16.7%, LABA −12.5%. The closer growth sits to the discount rate, the further away the cash, and the bigger the fall.\",\"wrong\":\"Compute 1 ÷ (r − g) before and after for each. The closer g is to r, the bigger the change.\",\"concept\":\"equity-duration\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"One of them, by hand\",\"lines\":[\"LABE, g = 5%. Before: 1 ÷ (0.08 − 0.05) = 33.33 times its cash.\",\"After: 1 ÷ (0.09 − 0.05) = 25.00 times its cash.\",\"Fall, in % = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":33.33,\"text\":\"That measures the drop against the new price. Measure it from the old one: 8.33 ÷ 33.33.\"},{\"value\":8.33,\"text\":\"8.33 is the drop in the multiple. As a share of 33.33, it's 25%.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: −25%. One point of rate took a quarter off LABE, with no change in its business.\",\"incorrect\":\"1 − 25.00 ÷ 33.33, in %.\"},\"after\":\"Same cash, same company, one point of rate: −25%. When a rate surprise hits, the stocks priced furthest into the future move most.\",\"concept\":\"equity-duration\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.3-1\",\"q-3.8.3-2\",\"q-3.8.3-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"P/E prices the shares; EV/EBITDA prices the whole business, so debt and cash count.\",\"A low multiple can mean slow growth or a value trap, not a bargain.\",\"The closer growth sits to the discount rate, the more a rate rise hits the price.\"]}},\"3.8.4\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.4\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A model values LABB at 1,968m vUSD with a 9% discount rate. Lower the rate to 8% and keep everything else the same. What is the new value?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":1500,\"max\":2600,\"step\":10,\"unit\":\"m\",\"start\":1970,\"answer\":2340,\"tolerance\":20,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":1500,\"to\":2050,\"text\":\"One point of rate moves a DCF far more than one percent. The value rises about 19%.\"},{\"from\":2060,\"to\":2310,\"text\":\"Right direction, too small. Most of the value sits in the terminal value, which is very rate-sensitive.\"},{\"from\":2370,\"to\":2600,\"text\":\"Too far. The new value is 2,337m, +18.8%.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You said {yours}. At 8% the same cash flows are worth 2,337m: +18.8% from one point of discount rate, with nothing about the business changed.\",\"concept\":\"discounted-cash-flow\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Five years and a tail\",\"lines\":[\"Free cash flow: 100, 110, 121, 133.1, 146.41m (10% growth a year).\",\"[[present-value|Present value]] of the five years at 9%: 467.2m.\",\"[[terminal-value|Terminal value]] = 146.41 × 1.025 ÷ (0.09 − 0.025) = 2,308.8m; discounted five years: 1,500.5m.\",\"Total: 467.2 + 1,500.5 = 1,967.8m.\",\"Terminal value's share of the total, in % = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":76,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":117,\"text\":\"That uses the undiscounted 2,308.8m. Use its present value, 1,500.5m.\"},{\"value\":24,\"text\":\"That's the five years' share. The rest is the terminal value.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 76%. Three quarters of this value is everything after year five.\",\"incorrect\":\"1,500.5 ÷ 1,967.8, in %.\"},\"after\":\"Over three quarters of the value rests on one assumption about the far future: growth of 2.5% a year, forever. That's normal for a [[dcf|DCF]], and it's why small changes move it so much.\",\"concept\":\"discounted-cash-flow\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Two assumptions, big swings\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"unit\":\"m\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"WACC 8%\",\"value\":2337,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"WACC 9% (base)\",\"value\":1968,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"WACC 10%\",\"value\":1697,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Terminal growth 1.5%\",\"value\":1755,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Terminal growth 2.5% (base)\",\"value\":1968,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"Terminal growth 3.5%\",\"value\":2258,\"role\":\"up\"}],\"steps\":[\"WACC 8%, 9%, 10%: 2,337m, 1,968m, 1,697m. One point either way moves the value 14–19%.\",\"Terminal growth 1.5%, 2.5%, 3.5%: 1,755m, 1,968m, 2,258m. One point moves it 11–15%.\"]},\"text\":\"[[wacc|WACC]], the discount rate, and terminal growth are the two least knowable inputs. One point in either moves the value more than most quarters' news.\",\"concept\":\"discounted-cash-flow\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"The market disagrees\",\"prompt\":\"The market values LABB at 2,600m, 632m above the model. What does the gap tell you first?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"short\",\"label\":\"LABB is overvalued: sell it short\",\"feedback\":\"That treats your model as the truth. The price may be right and your growth estimate too low.\"},{\"id\":\"implied\",\"label\":\"The price assumes faster growth than the model; find out how much\"},{\"id\":\"rate\",\"label\":\"The model is wrong: lower the rate until it matches\",\"feedback\":\"Bending the rate to fit the price hides the question. Ask what the price assumes, then judge that.\"}],\"answer\":\"implied\"},\"reveal\":\"Run the model in reverse: keep the 9% rate and 2.5% terminal growth, and find the five-year growth that gives 2,600m. That's a [[reverse-dcf]]. It turns 'the price is wrong' into a question you can judge: is that growth believable?\",\"concept\":\"implied-expectations\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Reverse the DCF\",\"lines\":[\"Keep WACC 9% and terminal growth 2.5%. Change only the five-year growth.\",\"Growth 15% a year: 2,304.6m. Growth 20% a year: 2,686.3m. The price, 2,600m, lies between.\",\"Interpolate: 15 + 5 × (2,600 − 2,304.6) ÷ (2,686.3 − 2,304.6) = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":18.9,\"tolerance\":0.4,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":10,\"text\":\"10% is the model's own growth, which gives 1,968m. The price needs more.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: about 18.9% a year. Solving the model exactly gives 18.9% too.\",\"incorrect\":\"15 + 5 × 295.4 ÷ 381.7.\"},\"after\":\"The price implies about 18.9% growth a year for five years, almost double the model's 10%. Not 'overvalued', but 'priced for 18.9%'. Now you can ask whether that's believable, and what would show it.\",\"concept\":\"implied-expectations\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"A model, not a forecast. A DCF to one decimal looks precise, but its inputs are guesses about the far future, and it says nothing about when price and model might meet. As a trading signal it's grade D. Watch for anchoring: once you've built a model, you'll want the market to agree with it.\"},{\"kind\":\"fields\",\"title\":\"Your view on LABB\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you'd have to believe. The price implies about 18.9% growth a year for five years.\",\"fields\":[{\"id\":\"view\",\"label\":\"Is 18.9% a year believable for LABB? Why?\",\"kind\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"placeholder\":\"e.g. Only if the new product doubles sales; nothing yet shows that\",\"required\":true},{\"id\":\"wrong\",\"label\":\"What would prove you wrong?\",\"kind\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"placeholder\":\"e.g. Two quarters of free cash flow growing faster than 18%\",\"required\":true}],\"correct\":\"Saved. You've turned a valuation into a testable view, with the evidence that would change your mind.\",\"concept\":\"implied-expectations\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.4-1\",\"q-3.8.4-2\",\"q-3.8.4-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"A DCF is five years of discounted free cash flow plus a terminal value.\",\"The terminal value is often most of the total; one point of WACC moves value 14–19%.\",\"Reverse the DCF: find the growth the price implies, then judge whether it's believable.\"]}},\"3.8.5\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.5\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABB beats the EPS consensus, 2.05 against 2.00. After the close it drops 6%. What is the most likely reason?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"algo\",\"label\":\"Algorithms misread the number\",\"feedback\":\"Headline algorithms do react first, but a misread reverses within minutes. A 6% drop that holds has a reason.\"},{\"id\":\"bar\",\"label\":\"A beat was expected; the real bar was higher\"},{\"id\":\"never\",\"label\":\"Earnings never move prices\",\"feedback\":\"This report moved LABB 6%. The puzzle is why a beat moved it down.\"},{\"id\":\"manip\",\"label\":\"Short sellers manipulated it\",\"feedback\":\"Nothing here points to that. A simpler reason fits: the market expected more than the consensus.\"}],\"answer\":\"bar\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. In the S&P 500, about 78% of companies beat the EPS consensus on average over five years, and about 86% in Q2 2026 (FactSet data). A beat is the usual case; the surprise's size and guidance decide the move.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-surprise\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"A beat is the base case\",\"text\":\"The [[consensus-estimate]] is the visible bar, and most companies clear it: an [[earnings-beat]] is the base case. The real bar can sit higher: a [[whisper-number]], or what buyers already paid for in a run-up. Judge a report by how big the surprise is against usual surprises, and by guidance (next lesson).\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Report day, step by step\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":93.46,\"h\":94.35,\"l\":93.06,\"c\":94.1},{\"o\":94.1,\"h\":95.1,\"l\":93.73,\"c\":94.85},{\"o\":94.85,\"h\":95.29,\"l\":94.34,\"c\":94.6},{\"o\":94.6,\"h\":95.75,\"l\":94.03,\"c\":95.4},{\"o\":95.4,\"h\":96.8,\"l\":94.94,\"c\":96.2},{\"o\":96.2,\"h\":96.69,\"l\":95.7,\"c\":96.05},{\"o\":96.05,\"h\":97.37,\"l\":95.71,\"c\":97.1},{\"o\":97.1,\"h\":98.6,\"l\":96.74,\"c\":98},{\"o\":98,\"h\":99.25,\"l\":97.55,\"c\":98.9},{\"o\":98.9,\"h\":100.35,\"l\":98.36,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.6,\"l\":87.2,\"c\":88.4},{\"o\":88,\"h\":89.3,\"l\":86.9,\"c\":88.7}]},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Ten days of run-up into the report: +7%, from 93.46 to 100.00.\",\"visible\":10,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"bracket\",\"from\":93.46,\"to\":100,\"index\":9,\"label\":\"+7%\",\"role\":\"up\"}]},{\"text\":\"The report lands after the close, at 100.00. The regular session is over.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":9,\"at\":\"close\",\"label\":\"Report\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},{\"text\":\"Extended hours: thin trading, wide spreads, limit orders only. Price swings to 87.20.\",\"visible\":11,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":10,\"at\":\"low\",\"label\":\"87.20\",\"role\":\"down\"}]},{\"text\":\"Next morning LABB opens at 88.00, far below a 95.00 stop.\",\"visible\":12,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":95,\"role\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"Stop 95.00\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":11,\"at\":\"open\",\"label\":\"Open 88.00\",\"role\":\"down\"}]}]},\"text\":\"A stop is an order, not a guarantee. It usually waits for the regular session, then fills at the first price there, wherever that is.\",\"concept\":\"gap-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Past the stop\",\"lines\":[\"Long LABB at 100.00, stop 95.00: 1R = 5.00 a unit.\",\"It opens at 88.00, and the stop fills there: a loss of 12.00 a unit.\",\"Loss in R = 12.00 ÷ 5.00 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":2.4,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1,\"text\":\"That's the plan. The gap skipped the stop, so the loss is 12.00, not 5.00.\"},{\"value\":1.4,\"text\":\"That's the extra loss beyond the plan. The whole loss is 2.4R.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 2.4R, before costs. The stop worked as an order; it just couldn't fill at 95.00.\",\"incorrect\":\"12.00 ÷ 5.00.\"},\"after\":\"Planned 1R, lost 2.4R. Nothing went wrong with the order: through a report, your real risk is the gap, not the stop distance.\",\"concept\":\"gap-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Size for the gap\",\"lines\":[\"Account 10,000, risk 1%: 1R = 100.00. A stop 5.00 away allows 19 units, both commissions included.\",\"LABB's largest gap in its synthetic history: 15.00, an open at 85.00.\",\"19 units there lose 285.00 + 1.76 of commissions = 286.76: 2.9R.\",\"Most whole units that keep that gap within 2R, 200.00, commissions included = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":13,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":14,\"text\":\"14 units lose 211.30 in that gap, commissions included: over 200.00.\"},{\"value\":6,\"text\":\"6 units keep the gap within 1R. The rule here allows 2R: 200.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 13 units lose 196.21 in that gap; 14 would lose 211.30.\",\"incorrect\":\"200.00 ÷ 15.00 = 13.3. Check that 13 units, with commissions, stay within 200.00.\"},\"after\":\"Holding through a report, size from the worst gap you plan for, not from the stop: 13 units, not 19. 'Gap ≤ 2R' accepts a bigger loss than 1R on rare days, never an unplanned one. Gaps beyond the worst one on record can still happen.\",\"concept\":\"gap-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Extended hours\",\"prompt\":\"Reports land when liquidity is thinnest. Match each extended-hours risk with what it does to you.\",\"columns\":[\"Risk\",\"What it does\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"No NBBO protection\",\"right\":\"You can fill worse than a price shown elsewhere\",\"wrong\":\"In regular hours, rules protect you from fills worse than the best displayed quote. In extended hours that protection doesn't apply.\"},{\"left\":\"Few participants\",\"right\":\"Wide spreads and big swings on small orders\",\"wrong\":\"With few traders in the book, each order moves price more, and spreads widen.\"},{\"left\":\"Brokers often accept limit orders only\",\"right\":\"Market orders and stops wait for the open\",\"wrong\":\"Many brokers allow only limits outside regular hours, so a stop waits for the regular session.\"},{\"left\":\"Options don't trade\",\"right\":\"No option hedge until the session opens\",\"wrong\":\"Options generally trade in regular hours only. Overnight, the hedge you'd want isn't available.\"}],\"concept\":\"gap-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Hold, cut or wait?\",\"prompt\":\"LABB reports tonight: implied move ±8%, run-up +7%, largest past gap 15.00. Does each plan keep the worst-case gap within 2R?\",\"columns\":[\"Plan\",\"Worst-case gap\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Go flat before the report\",\"right\":\"Within 2R\",\"wrong\":\"Flat means no gap risk at all. Missing a move is a cost you can afford.\"},{\"left\":\"Hold 13 units through the report\",\"right\":\"Within 2R\",\"wrong\":\"13 units lose 196.21 in a 15.00 gap: inside 200.00.\"},{\"left\":\"Wait, then enter after the report with a stop\",\"right\":\"Within 2R\",\"wrong\":\"Entering after the report, the gap is behind you. Your risk is the stop again.\"},{\"left\":\"Hold 19 units, stop at 95.00\",\"right\":\"Breaks the rule\",\"wrong\":\"The stop fills at the open. In a 15.00 gap, 19 units lose 286.76: 2.9R.\"},{\"left\":\"Hold 19 units, stop moved up to 97.00\",\"right\":\"Breaks the rule\",\"wrong\":\"A tighter stop doesn't help against a gap. The open still fills it far below.\"},{\"left\":\"Hold 19 units; sell after hours if it drops\",\"right\":\"Breaks the rule\",\"wrong\":\"After hours, liquidity is thin and orders are usually limit-only. A fill near your price isn't guaranteed.\"}],\"correct\":\"Three valid choices: flat, sized for the gap, or in after the report. Holding a normal-size position through the report breaks the rule.\",\"concept\":\"gap-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"info\",\"text\":\"Around reports, analysts make [[estimate-revision|estimate revisions]] and move price targets. Rating changes can move a stock for a while, but targets often follow the price rather than lead it (grade C). Read a target raised after a rally as a description of the rally, not a forecast.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.5-1\",\"q-3.8.5-2\",\"q-3.8.5-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"A beat is the base case; the surprise's size and guidance move price.\",\"Holding through a report, size from the worst gap: units within 2R at that gap.\",\"After hours: thin liquidity, limit-only orders, no option hedges; stops fill at the open.\"]}},\"3.8.6\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.6\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABA misses EPS by 3% but raises full-year guidance by 8%. Gap up, gap down, or a coin flip?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"up\",\"label\":\"Gap up\",\"feedback\":\"That's the lean: guidance moves future cash more than one quarter does. But the lean is slight, and the spread very wide.\"},{\"id\":\"down\",\"label\":\"Gap down\",\"feedback\":\"The miss pulls down, but the raise usually pulls harder. In the simulator this cell leaned slightly up.\"},{\"id\":\"flip\",\"label\":\"A coin flip\",\"feedback\":\"Close to the data: a slight lean up, with the widest spread of any cell.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You said {yours}. In 40 synthetic miss-and-raise reports, 22 gapped up and 17 down, from −14.1% to +13.8%: the widest spread in the grid. A slight lean up, too wide to call.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-guidance\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Median gap by cell\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"Beat + raise\",\"value\":4.9,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"In-line + raise\",\"value\":3.85,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"Beat + maintain\",\"value\":1.95,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"Miss + cut\",\"value\":-5.7,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Miss + maintain\",\"value\":-3.45,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"In-line + cut\",\"value\":-2.25,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Miss + raise\",\"value\":1.35,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"Beat + cut\",\"value\":0.1,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"In-line + maintain\",\"value\":0.6,\"role\":\"neutral\"}],\"steps\":[\"Aligned up: beat-and-raise, +4.9%, 35 of 40 gapped up. A raise or a beat alone leans up too.\",\"Aligned down: miss-and-cut, −5.7%, 39 of 40 gapped down. A miss or a cut alone leans down too.\",\"Mixed and no-news cells: medians near zero, with miss + raise spanning −14.1% to +13.8%.\"]},\"text\":\"Median overnight gap in each cell of the results × [[guidance]] matrix: 40 synthetic reports per cell, simulator design data. The real-world pattern is grade C, from practitioner sources; the simulator copies its shape, not its numbers.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-guidance\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Place the report\",\"prompt\":\"Six synthetic headlines. Place each one in its cell: results against consensus, then the new guidance against the old.\",\"columns\":[\"Headline\",\"Cell\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"LABA: EPS 2.12 vs 2.00 expected; full-year outlook raised to 8.80 from 8.40\",\"right\":\"Beat + raise\",\"wrong\":\"EPS above consensus, outlook up: the [[beat-and-raise]] corner.\"},{\"left\":\"LABD: EPS 1.40 vs 1.55 expected; outlook cut to 5.20 from 5.90\",\"right\":\"Miss + cut\",\"wrong\":\"Below consensus and a lower outlook: the miss-and-cut corner.\"},{\"left\":\"LABB: EPS 0.95 vs 0.88 expected; outlook cut to 3.40 from 3.70\",\"right\":\"Beat + cut\",\"wrong\":\"The quarter beat, but the outlook fell: a mixed cell.\"},{\"left\":\"LABE: EPS 0.50 vs 0.56 expected; outlook raised to 2.40 from 2.20\",\"right\":\"Miss + raise\",\"wrong\":\"The quarter missed, but the outlook rose: a mixed cell.\"},{\"left\":\"LABA: EPS 2.00, as expected; outlook unchanged at 8.80\",\"right\":\"In-line + maintain\",\"wrong\":\"Nothing new on either line: the centre of the grid.\"},{\"left\":\"LABD: EPS 1.55, as expected; outlook cut to 5.00 from 5.20\",\"right\":\"In-line + cut\",\"wrong\":\"Results matched; the outlook fell. The cut does the work.\"}],\"correct\":\"Read two comparisons: results against consensus, new guidance against old. The cell comes first; the story comes later.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-guidance\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Three twists\",\"text\":\"**[[sandbagging|Sandbagging]]**: a company that guides low by habit makes its raises expected, so a small raise disappoints. **Withdrawal**: in a crisis a company may pull its guidance, and the uncertainty itself moves price. **Two waves**: the gap on the release can reverse during the conference call, as management answers questions.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"The sandbagger\",\"prompt\":\"LABE has raised guidance at each of its last 8 reports, always by about 5%. Today it beats and raises by 2%. Which way does it gap?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"up\",\"label\":\"Up: beat-and-raise is the best cell\",\"feedback\":\"On paper, yes. But for LABE a raise is the habit, and 2% is less than the 5% traders expected.\"},{\"id\":\"flat\",\"label\":\"Flat: it's in line with history\",\"feedback\":\"History says 5%. A 2% raise is below LABE's own usual bar.\"},{\"id\":\"down\",\"label\":\"Down: the raise is smaller than expected\"}],\"answer\":\"down\"},\"reveal\":\"Down. A habitual sandbagger's raise is already in the price: the market expects its usual 5%. A 2% raise disappoints against that bar. The cell says beat-and-raise; the expectation says miss.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-guidance\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Your map of the grid\",\"prompt\":\"Predict the gap for all nine cells: up, down or uncertain. Use 'uncertain' where results and guidance disagree, or nothing changed.\",\"columns\":[\"Cell\",\"Gap\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Beat + raise\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"35 of 40 gapped up, median +4.9%: the clearest up cell.\"},{\"left\":\"Beat + maintain\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"32 of 40 gapped up, median +1.95%: the beat leans it up.\"},{\"left\":\"In-line + raise\",\"right\":\"Up\",\"wrong\":\"34 of 40 gapped up: the raise does the work.\"},{\"left\":\"Miss + cut\",\"right\":\"Down\",\"wrong\":\"39 of 40 gapped down, median −5.7%: the clearest down cell.\"},{\"left\":\"Miss + maintain\",\"right\":\"Down\",\"wrong\":\"34 of 40 gapped down, median −3.45%.\"},{\"left\":\"In-line + cut\",\"right\":\"Down\",\"wrong\":\"31 of 40 gapped down: the cut does the work.\"},{\"left\":\"Miss + raise\",\"right\":\"Uncertain\",\"wrong\":\"22 up, 17 down, from −14.1% to +13.8%: too wide to call.\"},{\"left\":\"Beat + cut\",\"right\":\"Uncertain\",\"wrong\":\"20 up, 19 down, from −13.3% to +8.5%: a coin flip whose worst gaps are down.\"},{\"left\":\"In-line + maintain\",\"right\":\"Uncertain\",\"wrong\":\"No new information: 23 up, 16 down, mostly small moves.\"}],\"correct\":\"Six cells lean clearly; three are flags, not forecasts. Size a flagged report as if anything can happen.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-guidance\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"info\",\"text\":\"The matrix is a map of tendencies, graded C: practitioner sources, with academic evidence on guidance not reviewed here. Every cell has outliers, and the bar is what the market expected, not the consensus alone. Write your map in your playbook and correct it against your own record.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.6-1\",\"q-3.8.6-2\",\"q-3.8.6-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Place a report on two lines: results against consensus, new guidance against old.\",\"Aligned cells lean clearly; mixed cells are wide coin flips: flag them uncertain.\",\"A habitual sandbagger's raise is expected, so a small raise can gap down.\"]}},\"3.8.7\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.7\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABB trades at 100.00. The at-the-money straddle for the expiry just after earnings costs 7.95. How big a move, up or down, is the market pricing?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":1,\"max\":20,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"start\":4,\"answer\":8,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":1,\"to\":6,\"text\":\"Higher. The straddle pays for any move beyond its cost: 7.95 on a 100.00 stock.\"},{\"from\":10,\"to\":20,\"text\":\"Lower. 7.95 ÷ 100.00 is just under 8%.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You said {yours}. Straddle ÷ price = 7.95 ÷ 100.00 ≈ 8%: the market prices a move of about ±8% either way through the report.\",\"concept\":\"implied-move\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Calls, puts and the straddle\",\"text\":\"A call is the right to buy at the strike price; a put, the right to sell. An option's price is its intrinsic value plus a premium for uncertainty, set by [[implied-volatility]]. A [[straddle]] buys both at the same strike: it pays if price moves far either way. Greeks and strategies come in 4.1.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Before and after the report\",\"lines\":[\"Before: 7 days left, implied volatility 72%. The call and the put cost 3.98 each (3.976 unrounded): straddle 7.95.\",\"The report: LABB rises 3%, to 103.00. Implied volatility drops to 30%, with 6 days left.\",\"The call is now worth 3.50: intrinsic 3.00 plus 0.50 of uncertainty.\",\"P&L on the call bought at 3.98 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-0.48,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.48,\"text\":\"Check the sign: you paid 3.98 and it's worth 3.50.\"},{\"value\":3,\"text\":\"That's the gain in intrinsic value. The call also lost most of its uncertainty premium.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: −0.48, or −12%, though you called the direction. The premium for uncertainty collapsed.\",\"incorrect\":\"3.50 − 3.98.\"},\"after\":\"Most of the 3.98 was paying for uncertainty. The report resolved it, implied volatility fell from 72% to 30%, and that premium vanished faster than the 3% rise could make up: [[iv-crush]].\",\"concept\":\"iv-crush\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"The straddle after the report\",\"prompt\":\"You bought the straddle for 7.95. LABB opens at 103.00 and implied volatility falls to 30%. Roughly what is the straddle worth now?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"11\",\"label\":\"About 11.00\",\"feedback\":\"That would need a big move and the old volatility. Both legs lost their uncertainty premium.\"},{\"id\":\"8\",\"label\":\"About 7.95\",\"feedback\":\"The 3% move is well under the 8% priced. The straddle can't hold its value.\"},{\"id\":\"4\",\"label\":\"About 4.00\"},{\"id\":\"3\",\"label\":\"About 3.00\",\"feedback\":\"Close: that's the call's intrinsic value. The two legs still hold about 0.99 of time value.\"}],\"answer\":\"4\"},\"reveal\":\"About 3.99: the call 3.50, the put 0.50. That's −50% on a 3% move. After the report, the straddle needs about ±7.9% just to get back to 7.95: the move it was pricing all along.\",\"concept\":\"iv-crush\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Where the call breaks even\",\"lines\":[\"After the report, the call is worth its intrinsic value, price − 100, plus a little time value.\",\"At 30% volatility with 6 days left, the time value near 103.60 is about 0.38.\",\"Break-even: price − 100 + 0.38 = 3.98. Price = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":103.6,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":103.98,\"text\":\"That ignores the 0.38 of time value still in the call.\"},{\"value\":103,\"text\":\"At 103.00 the call is worth 3.50, not 3.98. Price has to rise about 0.60 more.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: about 103.60, a 3.6% rise, just to break even.\",\"incorrect\":\"100 + 3.98 − 0.38.\"},\"after\":\"Below 103.60, LABB can rise and the call still loses. Buying calls before a report isn't a cheap bet on a beat: most of the premium pays for uncertainty, and the report takes it away.\",\"concept\":\"iv-crush\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Cheap or rich? Check the record\",\"lines\":[\"LABB's last 8 reports, implied move: 8.0, 7.5, 8.5, 9.0, 7.0, 8.0, 7.5, 8.5%. Average 8.0%.\",\"Actual moves: 6.6, 12.4, 3.0, 6.8, 2.6, 4.4, 4.6, 14.0%. Average 6.8%.\",\"Average actual ÷ average implied = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":0.85,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1.18,\"text\":\"That's implied over actual. Put the actual move on top.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 0.85. On average LABB moved 85% of what options priced.\",\"incorrect\":\"6.8 ÷ 8.0.\"},\"after\":\"Options were rich here on average: 6 of 8 moves were smaller than priced. But the 2 misses were 12.4% and 14.0%, against 7.5% and 8.5% priced. Eight reports is a tiny sample; compare many before calling options cheap or rich.\",\"concept\":\"implied-move\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"The evidence is mixed (grade C). In one dataset, actual moves averaged about 85% of implied; another study finds earnings options fairly priced in aggregate. Selling straddles wins often and loses rarely, but big: one tail move can erase many crushes. 'Straddles are expensive' is not a free trade.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.7-1\",\"q-3.8.7-2\",\"q-3.8.7-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Implied move ≈ at-the-money straddle ÷ share price: the move the market already prices.\",\"After the report, implied volatility collapses; a call can lose even when price rises.\",\"Compare realised and implied moves over many reports before calling options cheap or rich.\"]}},\"3.8.8\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.8\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"After big earnings surprises, prices tend to keep drifting the same way for weeks. Since the 1980s, has that drift grown, stayed the same, or shrunk?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"grown\",\"label\":\"Grown\",\"feedback\":\"More data and faster markets made it smaller, not bigger: once published, traders went after it.\"},{\"id\":\"same\",\"label\":\"Stayed the same\",\"feedback\":\"It hasn't held up. Published edges usually shrink, and this one shrank a lot.\"},{\"id\":\"shrunk\",\"label\":\"Shrunk\"}],\"answer\":\"shrunk\"},\"compare\":\"You said {yours}. The top-minus-bottom drift spread fell from about 5% in the 1980s–90s to 3% or less in the late 2010s, and Martineau (2021) finds no drift in large caps since about 2006.\",\"concept\":\"post-earnings-drift\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"How big was the surprise?\",\"lines\":[\"LABB reports EPS of 2.30; the expected figure was 2.00. Surprise: +0.30.\",\"Its past surprises usually varied by 0.12: their standard deviation.\",\"[[sue|SUE]] = 0.30 ÷ 0.12 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":2.5,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.3,\"text\":\"That's the raw surprise. Divide it by the usual size of surprises, 0.12.\"},{\"value\":0.15,\"text\":\"0.30 ÷ 2.00 is the surprise as a share of EPS. SUE divides by the usual surprise, 0.12.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 2.5. A surprise two and a half times the usual size.\",\"incorrect\":\"0.30 ÷ 0.12.\"},\"after\":\"Standardising makes surprises comparable across companies, like the standardised surprise for economic data in 2.10. Studies rank stocks by SUE and compare the top and bottom tenths, the deciles.\",\"concept\":\"earnings-surprise\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The drift, as first measured\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0}],\"hideCandles\":true,\"range\":[-3,3.2],\"describe\":\"Synthetic average cumulative return after the report day, days 0 to 60 in 5-day steps. Top SUE decile: 0 to +2.70%. Bottom decile: 0 to −2.30%. Spread at day 60: 5.0 points.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Top decile of surprises: after the report day, prices keep rising, +2.70% over 60 days.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":0,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"Report-day close\"},{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[0,0.48,0.89,1.23,1.52,1.77,1.97,2.15,2.3,2.43,2.53,2.62,2.7],\"role\":\"up\",\"label\":\"Top SUE\"}]},{\"text\":\"Bottom decile: prices keep falling, −2.30% over the same 60 days.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[0,-0.41,-0.75,-1.05,-1.29,-1.5,-1.68,-1.83,-1.96,-2.07,-2.16,-2.23,-2.3],\"role\":\"down\",\"label\":\"Bottom SUE\"}]},{\"text\":\"Spread at day 60: 5.0 points, as in the 1980s–90s. By the late 2010s, 3 or less.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"bracket\",\"from\":-2.3,\"to\":2.7,\"index\":12,\"label\":\"5.0\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]}]},\"text\":\"A synthetic illustration shaped like the early studies: the drift after the report, not the report-day jump. About 25–30% of the original drift came at the next quarters' reports, as the surprise kept surprising.\",\"concept\":\"post-earnings-drift\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Where it came from\",\"text\":\"[[pead|PEAD]] was first documented by Ball and Brown (1968). Bernard and Thomas (1989) found the top-minus-bottom SUE spread positive in 41 of 48 quarters, 1974–1985. The usual explanations: investors underreact to earnings news, and limits to arbitrage, such as costs and risk, stop traders closing the gap. Grade B, strongly decayed.\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Where it lives: small, illiquid stocks few analysts follow, exactly where spreads, impact and borrowing costs are highest. In large caps it's about zero since around 2006 (Martineau 2021). Across 97 published predictors, returns fell 26% out of sample and 58% after publication (McLean and Pontiff): an [[anomaly]] shrinks once it's known.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"True or false?\",\"prompt\":\"Four claims about PEAD. Sort them.\",\"columns\":[\"Claim\",\"Verdict\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"PEAD has been strongest in small, thinly followed stocks\",\"right\":\"True\",\"wrong\":\"The drift concentrates where few analysts look and trading is costly.\"},{\"left\":\"Part of the drift came at the next quarters' reports\",\"right\":\"True\",\"wrong\":\"About 25–30% of the drift appeared around the following reports.\"},{\"left\":\"In large caps, PEAD is still a reliable edge\",\"right\":\"False\",\"wrong\":\"Martineau (2021) finds no drift in large caps since about 2006.\"},{\"left\":\"The spread was positive in 41 of 48 quarters, so it will keep working\",\"right\":\"False\",\"wrong\":\"41 of 48 describes 1974–1985. Since publication, the effect has shrunk sharply.\"}],\"concept\":\"anomaly-decay\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Where the drift stops paying\",\"lines\":[\"Simulator, 8 years: buy the top SUE decile and hold 60 days. Gross return per trade: small caps +1.10%, large caps +0.15%.\",\"Commission: 0.05% each side, 0.10% a round trip.\",\"Small caps: the widest bid–ask spread, in % of price, at which the drift still breaks even = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":1,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1.1,\"text\":\"That forgets the 0.10% of commissions. They come out of the same 1.10%.\"},{\"value\":0.05,\"text\":\"That's the large-cap answer. Small caps start from +1.10%.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 1.00%. Any wider and the small-cap drift loses money.\",\"incorrect\":\"1.10 − 0.10.\"},\"after\":\"The simulator's small caps trade at a 1.20% spread: net −0.20% a trade. Large caps leave 0.05% for the spread, about what it costs, so net is about zero. The drift lives where costs eat it.\",\"concept\":\"anomaly-decay\"},{\"kind\":\"fields\",\"title\":\"Your stop rule\",\"prompt\":\"Before you trade any published edge, write when you'll stop. Make it checkable: a number, a window, a test.\",\"fields\":[{\"id\":\"rule\",\"label\":\"I stop trading the drift when…\",\"kind\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"placeholder\":\"e.g. The 2-year net expectancy's 95% interval includes zero\",\"required\":true}],\"correct\":\"Saved. A stop rule written before the results can't be bent by them.\",\"concept\":\"anomaly-decay\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.8-1\",\"q-3.8.8-2\",\"q-3.8.8-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"SUE = surprise ÷ the usual size of surprises; studies rank stocks by it.\",\"PEAD is grade B and strongly decayed: about zero in large caps since 2006.\",\"The drift survives where costs are highest; find the break-even cost before trading it.\"]}},\"3.8.9\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.9\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABD agrees to be bought for 50.00 cash per share. It trades at 47.00. If the deal fails, it falls back to 35.00. What deal-success probability does 47.00 imply?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"start\":50,\"answer\":80,\"tolerance\":0,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0,\"to\":50,\"text\":\"Too low. 47.00 is much closer to 50.00 than to 35.00, so the market thinks the deal is likely.\"},{\"from\":90,\"to\":100,\"text\":\"Too high. If the deal were near-certain, LABD would trade almost at 50.00.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You said {yours}. p × 50.00 + (1 − p) × 35.00 = 47.00 gives p = 12 ÷ 15 = 80%. The 3.00 left to gain is the market's price for a 20% chance of a 12.00 fall.\",\"concept\":\"corporate-actions\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Typical reactions\",\"prompt\":\"Match each event with its typical first reaction. Typical, not guaranteed.\",\"columns\":[\"Event\",\"Typical reaction\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Buyback announced\",\"right\":\"A modest rise that, on average, doesn't reverse\",\"wrong\":\"About 95% of buybacks are open-market programmes the company needn't complete, so the effect is modest (grade C).\"},{\"left\":\"[[secondary-offering|Secondary offering]]\",\"right\":\"A drop: dilution and extra supply\",\"wrong\":\"New shares split the profit more ways and add supply, so the price usually falls on the news.\"},{\"left\":\"Takeover bid\",\"right\":\"The target jumps toward the offer; the buyer often falls\",\"wrong\":\"The target trades near the offer, less a discount for deal risk; the buyer pays a premium.\"},{\"left\":\"[[index-inclusion|Index inclusion]]\",\"right\":\"Mechanical buying by index funds\",\"wrong\":\"Funds that track the index must buy the stock, whatever they think of it.\"},{\"left\":\"Insiders buying shares\",\"right\":\"Read as information about the company\",\"wrong\":\"Managers buying with their own money is read as a sign they see value.\"}],\"concept\":\"corporate-actions\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The deal odds in the price\",\"lines\":[\"Offer 50.00. Price 47.00. Price if the deal fails: 35.00.\",\"If it closes: +3.00. If it fails: −12.00.\",\"Fair price: p × 50.00 + (1 − p) × 35.00 = 47.00.\",\"p = (47 − 35) ÷ (50 − 35), in % = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":80,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":94,\"text\":\"47 ÷ 50 compares the price with the offer alone. The price if the deal fails matters too.\"},{\"value\":20,\"text\":\"That's the chance the deal fails. The question asks for success.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 80%. The 3.00 spread pays for a 20% chance of losing 12.00.\",\"incorrect\":\"12 ÷ 15, in %.\"},\"after\":\"[[merger-arbitrage|Merger arbitrage]] buys that spread: small, frequent gains and rare large losses when deals break. It's Expert-level practice (grade C); here, use it to read what a price believes.\",\"concept\":\"corporate-actions\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"A crash that isn't\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":107.6,\"h\":108.88,\"l\":106.96,\"c\":108.36},{\"o\":108.36,\"h\":109.8,\"l\":108.04,\"c\":109.24},{\"o\":109.24,\"h\":109.6,\"l\":108.72,\"c\":109.04},{\"o\":109.04,\"h\":110.52,\"l\":108.48,\"c\":109.88},{\"o\":109.88,\"h\":111.24,\"l\":109.2,\"c\":110.56},{\"o\":110.56,\"h\":111.36,\"l\":109.52,\"c\":110.2},{\"o\":110.2,\"h\":111.28,\"l\":109.8,\"c\":110.92},{\"o\":110.92,\"h\":112.04,\"l\":110.68,\"c\":111.72},{\"o\":111.72,\"h\":112.08,\"l\":111.4,\"c\":111.84},{\"o\":111.84,\"h\":112.88,\"l\":111.52,\"c\":112.12},{\"o\":112.12,\"h\":112.68,\"l\":111.32,\"c\":111.84},{\"o\":111.84,\"h\":112.64,\"l\":111.12,\"c\":112.36},{\"o\":112.36,\"h\":113.56,\"l\":112.16,\"c\":113.28},{\"o\":113.28,\"h\":113.64,\"l\":112.68,\"c\":113.36},{\"o\":113.36,\"h\":114.92,\"l\":112.76,\"c\":114.48},{\"o\":114.48,\"h\":115.12,\"l\":113.68,\"c\":114.16},{\"o\":114.16,\"h\":115.2,\"l\":113.64,\"c\":114.4},{\"o\":114.4,\"h\":115.52,\"l\":113.88,\"c\":115.08},{\"o\":115.08,\"h\":116.16,\"l\":114.44,\"c\":115.64},{\"o\":115.64,\"h\":117.16,\"l\":115,\"c\":116.4},{\"o\":116.4,\"h\":117.08,\"l\":115.76,\"c\":116.68},{\"o\":116.68,\"h\":117.44,\"l\":115.76,\"c\":116.24},{\"o\":116.24,\"h\":117.8,\"l\":115.96,\"c\":117.36},{\"o\":117.36,\"h\":118.6,\"l\":116.96,\"c\":118.08},{\"o\":118.08,\"h\":118.52,\"l\":117.72,\"c\":118.24},{\"o\":118.24,\"h\":118.8,\"l\":117.68,\"c\":118.4},{\"o\":118.4,\"h\":119.56,\"l\":117.76,\"c\":118.8},{\"o\":118.8,\"h\":120.08,\"l\":118.4,\"c\":119.56},{\"o\":119.56,\"h\":120.2,\"l\":119.32,\"c\":119.68},{\"o\":119.68,\"h\":120.32,\"l\":119.2,\"c\":120},{\"o\":30.1,\"h\":30.44,\"l\":29.94,\"c\":30.25},{\"o\":30.25,\"h\":30.53,\"l\":30.17,\"c\":30.4},{\"o\":30.4,\"h\":30.58,\"l\":30.18,\"c\":30.32},{\"o\":30.32,\"h\":30.64,\"l\":30.14,\"c\":30.55},{\"o\":30.55,\"h\":30.84,\"l\":30.37,\"c\":30.7},{\"o\":30.7,\"h\":30.76,\"l\":30.43,\"c\":30.62}]},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"LABA climbs from about 108 to 120.00 over 30 bars.\",\"visible\":30},{\"text\":\"Bar 31 opens at 30.10: a 4-for-1 split, not a 75% crash.\",\"visible\":36,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":30,\"at\":\"open\",\"label\":\"Split 4:1\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},{\"text\":\"The unadjusted 20-bar average follows the fake drop: 116.20 to 91.37 in six bars.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"ma\",\"period\":20,\"role\":\"concept\",\"label\":\"MA 20\"}]}]},\"text\":\"A [[stock-split]] changes the share count and the price, never the value. Unadjusted, the chart and every indicator built on it see a crash, and a backtest would trade it.\",\"concept\":\"adjusted-price-data\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Fix the history\",\"prompt\":\"To remove the fake crash, what do you do to LABA's price history?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"before\",\"label\":\"Divide every price before the split by 4\"},{\"id\":\"after\",\"label\":\"Multiply every price after the split by 4\",\"feedback\":\"The shape works, but today's real price disappears from the chart. Adjust the past, not the present.\"},{\"id\":\"delete\",\"label\":\"Delete the bar with the gap\",\"feedback\":\"The gap would still be there, one bar later: every earlier price is on the old share basis.\"},{\"id\":\"all\",\"label\":\"Divide every price on the chart by 4\",\"feedback\":\"Prices after the split are already on the new basis. Dividing them again breaks the series.\"}],\"answer\":\"before\"},\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":26.9,\"h\":27.22,\"l\":26.74,\"c\":27.09},{\"o\":27.09,\"h\":27.45,\"l\":27.01,\"c\":27.31},{\"o\":27.31,\"h\":27.4,\"l\":27.18,\"c\":27.26},{\"o\":27.26,\"h\":27.63,\"l\":27.12,\"c\":27.47},{\"o\":27.47,\"h\":27.81,\"l\":27.3,\"c\":27.64},{\"o\":27.64,\"h\":27.84,\"l\":27.38,\"c\":27.55},{\"o\":27.55,\"h\":27.82,\"l\":27.45,\"c\":27.73},{\"o\":27.73,\"h\":28.01,\"l\":27.67,\"c\":27.93},{\"o\":27.93,\"h\":28.02,\"l\":27.85,\"c\":27.96},{\"o\":27.96,\"h\":28.22,\"l\":27.88,\"c\":28.03},{\"o\":28.03,\"h\":28.17,\"l\":27.83,\"c\":27.96},{\"o\":27.96,\"h\":28.16,\"l\":27.78,\"c\":28.09},{\"o\":28.09,\"h\":28.39,\"l\":28.04,\"c\":28.32},{\"o\":28.32,\"h\":28.41,\"l\":28.17,\"c\":28.34},{\"o\":28.34,\"h\":28.73,\"l\":28.19,\"c\":28.62},{\"o\":28.62,\"h\":28.78,\"l\":28.42,\"c\":28.54},{\"o\":28.54,\"h\":28.8,\"l\":28.41,\"c\":28.6},{\"o\":28.6,\"h\":28.88,\"l\":28.47,\"c\":28.77},{\"o\":28.77,\"h\":29.04,\"l\":28.61,\"c\":28.91},{\"o\":28.91,\"h\":29.29,\"l\":28.75,\"c\":29.1},{\"o\":29.1,\"h\":29.27,\"l\":28.94,\"c\":29.17},{\"o\":29.17,\"h\":29.36,\"l\":28.94,\"c\":29.06},{\"o\":29.06,\"h\":29.45,\"l\":28.99,\"c\":29.34},{\"o\":29.34,\"h\":29.65,\"l\":29.24,\"c\":29.52},{\"o\":29.52,\"h\":29.63,\"l\":29.43,\"c\":29.56},{\"o\":29.56,\"h\":29.7,\"l\":29.42,\"c\":29.6},{\"o\":29.6,\"h\":29.89,\"l\":29.44,\"c\":29.7},{\"o\":29.7,\"h\":30.02,\"l\":29.6,\"c\":29.89},{\"o\":29.89,\"h\":30.05,\"l\":29.83,\"c\":29.92},{\"o\":29.92,\"h\":30.08,\"l\":29.8,\"c\":30},{\"o\":30.1,\"h\":30.44,\"l\":29.94,\"c\":30.25},{\"o\":30.25,\"h\":30.53,\"l\":30.17,\"c\":30.4},{\"o\":30.4,\"h\":30.58,\"l\":30.18,\"c\":30.32},{\"o\":30.32,\"h\":30.64,\"l\":30.14,\"c\":30.55},{\"o\":30.55,\"h\":30.84,\"l\":30.37,\"c\":30.7},{\"o\":30.7,\"h\":30.76,\"l\":30.43,\"c\":30.62}]},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Adjusted: bars 1–30 ÷ 4. The 20-bar average runs from 29.05 to 29.16 across the split.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"ma\",\"period\":20,\"role\":\"concept\",\"label\":\"MA 20\"}]}]},\"reveal\":\"Divide everything before the event, change nothing after it. The adjusted history shows LABA's real path: a steady climb, no crash. Most charting tools adjust for you; check whether your data source does before a backtest trusts it.\",\"concept\":\"adjusted-price-data\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Dividends and the ex-date\",\"text\":\"On the [[ex-dividend-date]], the price usually drops by about the dividend: value moves from the share into your cash. In the US, with T+1 settlement since May 2024, the ex-date is the record date for regular dividends. So buying just before it and selling after isn't free money: the drop, taxes and costs come first.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Adjust for both\",\"lines\":[\"LABA's last 50 bars: a 4-for-1 split at bar 31, a 0.80 dividend at bar 40.\",\"Split: every price before bar 31 ÷ 4. Bar 30's close: 120.00 ÷ 4 = 30.00.\",\"Dividend: bar 39 closed at 32.00, so every price before bar 40 × (1 − 0.80 ÷ 32.00) = × 0.975.\",\"Bar 30's fully adjusted close = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":29.25,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":30,\"text\":\"That's the split only. The dividend factor, × 0.975, applies to bar 30 too.\"},{\"value\":117,\"text\":\"That's the dividend factor on the unsplit price. Divide by 4 as well.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 29.25. Each adjustment applies to every bar before its event.\",\"incorrect\":\"30.00 × 0.975.\"},\"after\":\"Adjust everything before an event, nothing after it. Then a 20-bar average runs through bar 31 without a false crash, and a backtest doesn't trade a split. Use adjusted data for signals and actual prices for fills.\",\"concept\":\"adjusted-price-data\"}],\"check\":[\"q-3.8.9-1\",\"q-3.8.9-2\",\"q-3.8.9-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Buybacks lift modestly, offerings drop, targets jump toward the bid, index adds draw fund buying.\",\"A merger spread prices deal odds: p = (price − fail price) ÷ (offer − fail price).\",\"Adjust every price before a split or dividend, none after, or charts show fake gaps.\"]}}}");
